[Music]  the life-changing coronavirus pandemic  is making a lot of people think about  changing their working lives in one  survey by the pew research center  about two-thirds of unemployed adults  say they've considered making a career  change  one such person is christian simone of  minneapolis  she reinvented her career after she was  laid off  it was definitely a shocker  but in the same breath it was a blessing  in disguise as well when christian  simone  was laid off in december at her job as a  corporate project manager  she had an awakening it really just put  a hard focus on  me looking in the mirror and saying are  you really living  or are you just existing coping with  unemployment  the pandemic and the george floyd  protests in her city  she knew it was time for a change i  applied to grad school to get my  master's in social work  i want to go for my goals of becoming a  therapist focus on black women and women  of color  while saving for grad school christian  is also using  extra time at home to work on her beauty  and wellness  youtube channel skincare beauty  is a form of meditation i really wanted  to  just have something beautiful in a very  dark time  spring fashion 2020 has exited the chat  and be a constant reminder that you  deserve beauty as well  losing her job has meant a strict budget  and  relying on unemployment checks but  christian  is eager for her fresh start when i went  to work  i focused on the fact that you got to  pay your bills so you got to do what you  have to do  but to actually show up as myself my  authentic self  is the most liberating  yeah that's such a profound question are  you really living or are you just  existing a lot of folks have asked  themselves that in the last year so  cbs news business analyst jill  schlesinger joins us jill good morning  if someone is thinking about a career  change  where do they start well of course  use christian's advice which is you got  to pay your bills but beyond that  you really want to start researching  what your career is going to be looking  like in the future so  is it your industry is it another  industry and do that work and spend the  time  next well you really got to dust off the  old resume and the cover letters  yes we still use them they still are the  point of entry  don't forget that you've got to actually  practice those zooms it's different  being on a zoom in your existing  workplace than with new people  and of course be careful about your  social profiles because  those are going to be examined by any  future  employers so be careful there which part  of the job market's actually growing  right now  jill well as you would imagine  technology and e-commerce just exploded  i think  every day we hear about new warehouse  opportunities but there are some in  upper level management as well  you also want to look at healthcare  because telemedicine is going to become  massive online learning a really  interesting  area it's really growing and of course  close to our hearts digital  entertainment  many companies looking to beef up their  streaming services and the people who  can  help them get there a lot of people like  christian jill as you know  figured out a way to get a side hustle  that gives you some some income  so when do you know to make the decision  from letting the side  making the side hustle your full-time  job you know we've always heard these  funny stories about someone says oh i'm  a cook  i want to open a b don't do that okay do  exactly what christian is doing which is  you practice what that hobby that  avocation is you see if you can make  money  and you can slowly make the transition  when you can afford to do so  interesting fact here in the first year  of the pandemic so going back to last  february  through the next 12 months we saw 4.4  million  new businesses be created that's a 41  increase from the previous year now some  of this is necessity  but a lot of people are saying you know  what i want to be my own boss  and i can actually do it or they've all  they've said to themselves  i've always wanted to do i've always  wanted to be and now is a good time to  do something like that  right jill if you're really serious  about becoming self-employed what are  the benefits that you need to make sure  that you have this is a  critical question so uh thanks to the  new anchor for  joining in on this one the health  health insurance is obviously the most  important thing now the affordable care  act  has an open enrollment period right now  through  the middle of august you may if you were  laid off be entitled to cobra  from the government through september so  those health insurance plans  extremely important how about a  retirement plan maybe you had a nice  401k  through your employer you don't have  that anymore you can open an ira  a roth ira as your business builds a sep  ira  and many people have life and disability  insurance through their workplace  you may want to check before you make  the move whether you can pull that into  your  next career it's called portability you  want to see if you can take your  coverage  pay for it yourself it may be cheaper  than going out and replacing that  coverage  in your next iteration salary very  important jill how do you go about  negotiating a salary in a new industry  i feel like we need lisa demour here to  help us  first of all you really want to do again  let's go back to the research the  research is important because you want  to know what is this  paying in for for my position and you  want to compare  apples to apples right if you are making  50  000 in one job you're moving to a new  industry you might have to take a step  back  so you find out the salary range now you  then say  in the interview you say so what's the  range for this position gail king that  you're hiring for  and you give me the range and you say oh  it's a forty to fifty thousand and you  say  great i say the end of that  at the high end of that range at 50 so  you do say go for the highest  you do say go for the high end not in  the middle okay okay  yeah because behaviorally what the  employer is going to do is anchor  that number meaning that's the number  they're going to think they have to pay  you to gain  all right thank you jill schlesinger we  appreciate it we'll be right back stay  with us

